†Sign up】\nL111: \nL112: Appearance settings\nL113: You signed in with another tab or window. Reload to refresh your session. You signed out in another tab or window. Reload to refresh your session. You switched accounts on another tab or window. Reload to refresh your session. Dismiss alert\nL114: \nL115:   1. 【61†LayerZero-Labs】\nL116:   2. 【62†akita】\nL117: ## Repository navigation\nL118: \nL119:   *   * 【62†Code】\nL120:   * 【63†Issues8 (8)】\nL121:   * 【64†Pull requests25 (25)】\nL122: Only collaborators can create PRs\nL123:   * 【65†Actions】\nL124:   * 【66†Projects】\nL125:   * 【67†Security and quality】\nL126:   * 【68†Insights】\nL127: \nL128: More items\nL129: \nL130: # Tracking: consolidate Akita sumcheck into Jolt and complete native Spongefish integration #61\nL131: \nL132: New issue\nL133: \nL134: Copy link\nL135: \nL136: New issue\nL137: \nL138: Copy link\nL139: \nL140: Open\nL141: \nL142: Open\nL143: \nL144: 【69†Tracking: consolidate Akita sumcheck into Jolt and complete native Spongefish integration】#61\nL145: \nL146: Copy link\nL147: ## Description\nL148: \nL149: 【70†quangvdao】\nL150: \nL151: opened 【71†on Sep 23, 2026】\nL152: \nL153: Issue body actions\nL154: ## Objective\nL155: \nL156: Consolidate Akita's reusable sumcheck machinery into `jolt-poly`, `jolt-sumcheck`, and the existing native `jolt-transcript` integration, then complete the Jolt–Akita dependency cutover. The endpoint is one canonical implementation per shared concept, no `akita-sumcheck` compatibility facade, and working Jolt clear, committed/ZK, and Akita-backed paths.\nL157: Target the native Spongefish architecture being introduced by 【72†#37】. Do not build a new legacy transcript or structured-proof pipeline that the native migration is deliberately removing.\nL158: \nL159: This is an implementation tracker. The checkboxes below are acceptance work, not claims of completed validation.\nL160: ## Inspected baseline and prerequisites\nL161: \nL162: The source inspection used:\nL163:   * Akita main: 【73†`a5f3629b1aa10ed7dad501f616ffab3e0ad94060`】.\nL164:   * Jolt main: 【74†`af14aff3987851dfa9c12f53ec91130e98ea9274`】.\nL165:   * Akita 【72†Feat/spongefish #37】: inspected head `6c0e0c4c025f9a451a45dec581a3076992a11638`, based on `cdb2d8430d09db0fc8715c8af8c67f578bfcc5fc`. Reinspect the actual landed result before extraction.\nL166:   * Jolt currently pins Akita `252abb895046cc1d5b9955a26a2ad2318148ac26`; this predates the owning CPU-backend split on Akita main.\nL167:   * Akita 【72†Feat/spongefish #37】 uses Spongefish `ef9741346a150039427d9e0c02d6b2d73e93ec81`; inspected Jolt main uses `d2d190b1329d35ac9577438d05aed4f17a57b9f9`. Align on the agreed revision before sharing native state/codec types.\nL168: Jolt's standalone sumcheck and minimal `Field` boundary already provide the foundation: a dependency-light clear engine, `BatchPrelude`, `ProveRounds`, `RoundScheduler`, separate clear/committed recorders, and optional R1CS lowering. See 【75†the standalone contract】.\nL169: Downstream constraint: Akita's generic batched driver has external consumers even though it has no current production callers inside Akita. Internal call-site searches are not sufficient grounds to remove its capabilities. Keep those consumers pinned until an equivalent shared API is available and their migration is validated; do not add permanent forwarding aliases as a substitute for the cutover.\nL170: ## Ownership after migration\nL171: Owner  | Responsibilities\nL172: --- | ---\nL173: `jolt-field`  | Canonical field types and arithmetic capabilities\nL174: `jolt-poly`  | Ordinary full/compressed univariate representations and shared polynomial arithmetic; normalized inner-polynomial representation where appropriate\nL175: `jolt-sumcheck`  | Checked round shapes, ordinary and normalized claim transitions, generic proving/replay, batching geometry, scheduler integration, reduction results\nL176: `jolt-transcript` / Spongefish  | Typed native message codecs and prover/verifier operations; shared checked verifier-state behavior and reusable sampling primitives\nL177: Akita protocol crates  | PCS relations, stage order, setup/schedules, grinding plans, protocol/session/instance binding, terminal equations, diagnostics\nL178: `akita-cpu-backend`  | Specialized arithmetic, opaque sessions, compact witness storage, prefix kernels, source-specific optimizations\nL179: Jolt protocol crates  | VM relations and claim ownership, stage recipes, commitment policy, BlindFold integration, Akita PCS adapter\nL180: Shared Jolt crates must not depend on Akita. A native clear stream must not pull commitment, curve, R1CS, or prover-only dependencies into the minimal verifier path.\nL181: ## Work packages and acceptance gates\nL182: ### 1. Establish the post-native integration baseline\nL183:   * [Input] Reconcile 【72†Feat/spongefish #37】 with the newer owning CPU backend and fallible `SumcheckKernel` changes. Its inspected native prover still uses the older infallible instance interface; preserve errors from round generation, binding, and finalization as well as release-mode round-identity checks.\nL184:   * [Input] Align Jolt's Spongefish revision and review actual API changes. A dependency bump alone does not migrate `jolt-sumcheck` off its legacy transcript surface.\nL185:   * [Input] Inventory Jolt's stale CPU imports and custom polynomial-source contracts against current Akita. Keep the backend adapter refresh reviewable separately from sumcheck algebra changes.\nL186:   * [Input] Record accepted native proof grammar, challenge distributions, field/extension configurations, and representative correctness/performance baselines at exact commits.\nL187: Gate: the extraction target is the actual reconciled native implementation, not the pre-native code or an older integration pin.\nL188: ### 2. Consolidate polynomial arithmetic\nL189:   * [Input] Replace duplicate ordinary `UniPoly` / `CompressedUniPoly` arithmetic with canonical Jolt types and direct imports.\nL190:   * [Input] Specify constant/zero polynomial compression, empty-message rejection, degree accounting, coefficient access, and high-zero padding. Jolt's current primitive compression and Akita's differ on constants/empty zero polynomials.\nL191:   * [Input] Preserve an appropriate equispaced interpolation algorithm; avoid accidentally replacing a Newton-difference hot path with a more expensive general matrix solve.\nL192:   * [Input] Add the normalized equality-factored representation as a distinct type: it stores `[q1, ..., qd]`, unlike ordinary compression `[c0, c2, ..., cd]`.\nL193:   * [Input] Move compact fold LUTs and signed accumulator helpers to their actual arithmetic/backend owner; do not move them into the generic protocol crate merely to empty `akita-sumcheck`.\nL194: Gate: one owner per arithmetic concept; algebraic tests cover representation edge cases and measured hot paths do not regress without explanation.\nL195: ### 3. Share typed native operations and explicit challenge policies\nL196:   * [Input] Extend the existing native prover/verifier surface rather than introduce a third arbitrary-byte transcript facade.\nL197:   * [Input] Accept borrowed native state so a sumcheck can compose within an enclosing protocol. Keep prover and verifier roles distinct, field/extension relationships explicit, and message shapes checked before allocation.\nL198:   * [Input] Preserve typed errors from the protocol engine, backend, decoding, and scheduled challenge operations. Do not stringify errors or add Jolt-to-Akita dependencies.\nL199:   * [Input] Share exact field/extension sampling primitives where appropriate while keeping nonzero sampling, shortened challenges, and grinding explicit policies. A codec for one fixed representation is not a replacement for variable-attempt rejection sampling.\nL200:   * [Input] Preserve 【72†Feat/spongefish #37】's permanent invalidation after receipt failures and explicitly invalidating semantic failures, fallible later challenge access, and EOF rejection after ignored failures; propagate all other protocol errors.\nL201:   * [Input] Keep diagnostic site/context metadata distinct from cryptographic domain separation. Do not begin absorbing diagnostic records as an incidental refactor.\nL202:   * [Input] Preserve the Keccak rejection-attempt-history behavior required by the selected Spongefish implementation.\nL203: Gate: typed native streams work for base and extension fields with both ordinary and scheduled challenge generation, without weakening verifier failure behavior or the standalone dependency boundary.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGPU-Accelerated Big Integer Arithmetic and Number Theoretic Transform for Cryptography in NVIDIA cuPQC 0.6 - cuPQC SDK Developer Hub (https://nvidia.github.io/cuPQC/releases/v0.6/)\nciteturn7view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://nvidia.github.io/cuPQC/releases/v0.6/\",\"pattern\":\"August\"}); Total lines: 795\nL0: [Input] [Input]\nL1: \nL2: 【0†Skip to content】\nL3: \nL4: 【1†Image: NVIDIA】 【2†Image: NVIDIA】 cuPQC SDK Developer Hub\nL5: \nL6: 【3†Home】 【4†Get Started】 【5†Applications】 【6†Blog】 【7†Docs】\nL7: \nL8: [Input] [Input]\nL9: \nL10: 【8†SDK v0.6.0†developer.nvidia.com】 【9†Sample Applications†github.com】\nL11: \nL12: [Input] 【3†Home】 【4†Get Started】 【5†Applications】 【6†Blog】 【7†Docs】\nL13: \nL14: 【8†SDK v0.6.0†developer.nvidia.com】 【9†Sample Applications†github.com】\nL15: \nL16: Categories\nL17: \nL18: 【6†All posts7】 【10†Release Blog3】 【11†Technical Blog2】 【12†Ecosystem2】\nL19: \nL20: Archive\nL21: 【6†All years7】 【13†20265】 【14†20251】 【15†20241】\nL22: # GPU-Accelerated Big Integer Arithmetic and Number Theoretic Transform for Cryptography in NVIDIA cuPQC 0.6\nL23: \nL24: August 28, 2026 cuPQC SDK v0.6\nL25: We're excited to announce cuPQC 0.6, delivering Big Integer Arithmetic (BigInt) and the Number Theoretic Transform (NTT) as foundational math layer primitives specifically built for cryptographic application developers working on NVIDIA GPUs. These libraries are engineered to power the entire cryptographic spectrum, from classical protocols and post-quantum cryptography (PQC) to the massive computational workloads at the core of Zero-Knowledge Proofs (ZKPs) and Fully Homomorphic Encryption (FHE).\nL26: ## What's New in 0.6\nL27: Whether you are scaling traditional public-key cryptography like RSA and ECC, implementing post-quantum schemes, or bringing advanced privacy-preserving technologies like ZKPs and FHE to production, you face the same bottleneck: executing millions of multi-precision integer operations and polynomial multiplications at high throughput.\nL28: Historically, accelerating these workloads on GPUs meant implementing and optimizing custom CUDA kernels from scratch for every specific ring dimension, bit width, and parameter set your schemes require.\nL29: cuPQC 0.6 eliminates this barrier by providing that arithmetic through the cuPQC-BigInt and cuPQC-NTT libraries as ready-to-use GPU primitives. You can now compose complex cryptographic protocols using accelerated multi-precision integer arithmetic and polynomial multiplication as standard building blocks.\nL30: These libraries are designed as device extensions, enabling you to fuse these arithmetic operations with your own specialized functions into a single kernel. This avoids unnecessary host-device data transfers and intermediate memory allocations, a critical requirement for applications like ZKP proving, FHE ciphertext evaluation, and large-scale public-key infrastructure.\nL31: Batch-oriented APIs enable thousands of concurrent operations to execute in parallel, letting you build faster, more scalable cryptographic solutions while focusing on your protocol design rather than arithmetic implementation.\nL32: This release adds:\nL33:   * cuPQC-BigInt: Provides fixed-width unsigned integers with full arithmetic up to 4096 bits and storage up to 8192 bits, with thread and warp execution models, Barrett and Montgomery reduction paths, and compile-time error-handling policies. It serves as the workhorse for classical cryptography, the classical halves of hybrid PQC protocols, and large-field ZKP arithmetic.\nL34:   * cuPQC-NTT: Provides cyclic forward and inverse transforms in the Montgomery domain, in both standard and staged execution modes, for transform lengths up to 2^{24} points over a user-selected prime up to 62 bits. This provides the exact heavy lifting required for the massive polynomial rings used in FHE and ZKP systems.\nL35: In our benchmarks, cuPQC-NTT runs 32-bit transforms 12× to 42× faster than a 28-core CPU across sizes from 2^{8} to 2^{24} points, and cuPQC-BigInt runs 34× to 773× faster than a single CPU core running GMP across the eight operations we measured.\nL36: These additions expand the cuPQC SDK into four libraries across two tiers. At the math layer, cuPQC-BigInt and cuPQC-NTT provide low-level arithmetic, while cuPQC-Hash delivers key ZKP primitives like Poseidon2 and Merkle trees. Together, they give developers full flexibility to either deploy ready-to-use algorithms or construct custom, high-performance cryptographic pipelines on GPUs.\nL37: The following sections walk through each library in detail, covering supported operations, key configuration parameters, and benchmark results.\nL38: ## cuPQC-BigInt: Multi-Precision Integer Arithmetic\nL39: Modern cryptographic algorithms work with numbers that are orders of magnitude larger than what standard processor types can handle natively. Zero-Knowledge Proofs operate over 256- to 384-bit scalar fields, ECC works over 256- to 512-bit prime fields, post-quantum isogeny schemes use primes in the 400- to 700-bit range, and traditional RSA keys span up to 4096 bits.\nL40: In implementations, these values are held in multi-precision representations, with modular arithmetic applied at full width throughout the protocol. When thousands of these operations must run concurrently at high throughput, carrying the load on CPUs alone quickly becomes an expensive bottleneck.\nL41: cuPQC-BigInt provides these values as fixed-width unsigned integers built from 32-bit little-endian limbs. Widths are multiples of 32. Full arithmetic is available up to 4096 bits, and the double-width type used for products stores up to 8192 bits. This single library covers standard key sizes, elliptic curve fields, and large ZKP proof-system fields without requiring a separate implementation for each.\nL42: The specific widths you can instantiate depend on how many threads cooperate on a single integer, as detailed in Table 2. Just like the native integer types they extend, arithmetic wraps modulo 2^{bit_width}.\nL43: A full product of two N-bit values is up to 2N bits, so `mul_wide` and `square` return a separate double-width type carrying twice the limbs, complete with `lo` and `hi` accessors for its halves. At the 4096-bit arithmetic limit that product is 8192 bits. That wide value is also what Barrett and Montgomery reductions take as input to reduce it back to a single width.\nL44: When you only need part of a product, `mul_low` and `mul_high` return a single width directly, and `mul_low` costs roughly half as much as a full wide multiply.\nL45: ### Operations\nL46: \nL47: The table below is a high-level view of the functions and operators. Overloads, execution-model restrictions, and full signatures are in 【16†Supported Operations†docs.nvidia.com】 in the cuPQC-BigInt Features guide.\nL48: \nL49: cuPQC-BigInt Operations\nL50: \nL51: `name()` = callable · `+` = operator\nL52: \nL53: Capability Functions and operators\nL54: \nL55:   * 01\nL56: \nL57: Construction and access\nL58: \nL59: Build a value from scalars or limbs, read parts of it back, and write it to global memory.\nL60: \nL61: `bigint()`from a 32- or 64-bit scalar, or a limb array\nL62: `store()`whole value, or one instance of